    "q-336703": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336703",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Let's substitute ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$ from $\\text{L1}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ into the initial system of ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into $\\text{initial system of ODEs}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{pre-multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
