    "q-337290": {
      "dom_id": "step-q337290",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2t-3$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">.</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-(-{e}^{t})\\cdot 2{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{4}{3}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4}{3}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t}(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{8}{3}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{8}{3}t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{8}{3}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t}(2t-1) \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1)-8(t+1) \\\\ 2(2t-1)+8(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2t-3 \\\\ 4t+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
